Hello <%= @user.login %>,

Your VPS <%= @vps.id %> <%= @vps.hostname %> was stopped after repeated resource incidents.

  Incident type: <%= @incident_type_label %>
  Incidents in the last <%= @warning_period_days %> days: <%= @incident_count_7d %> (warning limit: <%= @warning_limit %>)
  Incidents in the last <%= @stop_period_days %> days: <%= @incident_count_30d %> (stop limit: <%= @stop_limit %>)
  Incident time: <%= local_time(@incident_at, '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S %Z') %>
  Stopped at: <%= local_time(@stopped_at, '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S %Z') %>

This resource pressure affects other VPS on the same node and may disrupt their
services. Please review your VPS's memory usage and service configuration. A
common cause is a web server or application spawning more worker processes than
the available memory can support. Reducing the maximum number of workers may
resolve the problem.

If your workload needs more memory, you can increase your VPS's memory.

The automatic stop also disabled autostart. You can start the VPS again in
vpsAdmin to adjust its configuration.
<% if @stop_enforced -%>
If another incident occurs while the total remains at or above <%= @stop_limit %> within the last
<%= @stop_period_days %> days, it will be stopped again, at most once per <%= @repeat_stop_hours %> hours. Once the total
falls below this limit, the usual limits apply again.
<% end -%>

Incident history in vpsAdmin:

  <%= @base_url %>?page=incidents&action=list&list=1&vps=<%= @vps.id %>&codename=oomd

